From c73a6733b5bb788d02a206211c72341c4b46eff5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 03:44:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: the video of a picture travels with it, not read
 from the video of now when it is drawn

The automatic choice of the content mapping v4.0 is made once a video, and kept for that video
(dvbridge_set_cm40_video()). The bridge took the number of the video from the count of
DVBRIDGE::NewVideo() when it drew a frame. NewVideo() runs on the player's thread, in
CVideoPlayer::Prepare(), when a file is opened while another plays; the renderer is flushed
asynchronously and draws the last picture of the video before again until then. A frame of that
video drawn in that window made the decision "of the next video" with its own RPU, and the next
video never made its own: where the first said no and the next has no trims of the colourist, the
next went out without the default v4.0 part for all of it, and nothing in the log said so.

Now the number travels with the picture (VideoPicture::doviVideo). The decoder takes it
(DVBRIDGE::VideoNumber()) with the first packet that comes after it was opened or reset by the
player, in order with the packets of the stream: the player opens the next video after it flushed
this one, and its packets come after that. Resets of the decoder's own within a stream (after a
drain, at the end of the stream, when the hardware decoder flushed: ResetWithin()) keep it. The
renderer copies it to the bridge's layer (CDVBridgeGLES::Layer::video), and the bridge gives it to
the decision (dvbridge_cm40_video_of(): the number of the picture, or the video of now for a picture
without one and for the menus). The line of the layers of a video (DVBridgeLayers.h) is keyed on it
too.

What it decides is unchanged otherwise. cm40_test has a case that reproduces it (I: the last picture
of video 1 drawn after video 2 opened is of video 1; the first picture of video 2 decides for it),
and two mutations of dvbridge_cm40_video_of() that make it fail; the serializer gives the same bytes
for every frame of its test.

Found in a second review of these fixes.
